  "name": "Question 20",
  "problem": "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1-x)-2x}{x+1}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1-x)-2x)=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(x+1)=-\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{-\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1-x)-2x}{x+1} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1-x)-2x]^{\u2032}}{{[x+1]}^{\u2032}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{1-x}-2)}{1} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{1-x}+2) \\\\  & =-(0+2) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
